/**
@page troubleshooting Troubleshooting Guide

@tableofcontents

This page lists the runtime errors developers see most often when working
with @c database_system, with concrete fixes. If your problem is not
listed here, see @ref faq or open an issue at
https://github.com/kcenon/database_system/issues.

@section ts_connect Connection Failures

@subsection ts_connect_refused Symptom: connect_result returns "connection refused"

The driver could not reach the database host or the credentials were
rejected. Check, in order:

1. **Is the server running?** @c pg_isready -h @c localhost,
   @c systemctl @c status @c mysql, @c mongo --eval @c "db.runCommand('ping')".
2. **Is the host/port correct?** Compare your connection string against
   the server's bind address.
3. **Are the credentials valid?** Try the same connection string with
   the native CLI (@c psql, @c mysql, @c mongosh).
4. **Is the backend compiled in?** @c set_mode() returns @c false if the
   backend was not built. Re-run CMake with @c -DUSE_POSTGRESQL=ON or the
   equivalent flag for your backend.
5. **TLS/SSL mismatch?** PostgreSQL refuses connections when the server
   requires SSL but the client did not request it. Add
   @c sslmode=require to the connection string.

@code{.cpp}
auto r = mgr->connect_result(conn);
if (!r.is_ok())
{
    std::cerr << "code=" << r.error().code
              << " message=" << r.error().message << "\n";
}
@endcode

@subsection ts_connect_timeout Symptom: connection hangs forever

A firewall is silently dropping the SYN packet, or the host name resolves
to an unreachable address. Set an explicit timeout in the connection
string (@c connect_timeout=5 for libpqxx, @c connectTimeoutMS=5000 for
mongo-cxx-driver) so failures surface quickly, and verify the route with
@c nc -vz @c host @c port.

@section ts_transactions Transaction Deadlocks and Locking Errors

@subsection ts_tx_deadlock Symptom: "deadlock detected" or "Lock wait timeout exceeded"

Two transactions are waiting on each other's locks. The fix is one or
more of:

- **Acquire locks in a deterministic order.** If transaction A always
  updates @c users before @c orders, transaction B must do the same.
- **Shorten the transaction.** Move read-only work outside @c BEGIN /
  @c COMMIT, and avoid user-input prompts inside a transaction.
- **Retry on conflict.** Wrap the transaction in a loop that retries up
  to N times when @c result.error() reports a serialization or deadlock
  code.
- **Lower isolation when safe.** Switch from @c SERIALIZABLE to
  @c READ @c COMMITTED if your invariants allow it.

@code{.cpp}
for (int attempt = 0; attempt < 3; ++attempt)
{
    transaction_guard tx(mgr);
    auto r = do_work(mgr);
    if (r.is_ok()) { tx.commit(); break; }
    if (!is_retryable(r.error())) throw std::runtime_error(r.error().message);
}
@endcode

@subsection ts_tx_busy SQLite-specific: SQLITE_BUSY / "database is locked"

SQLite serialises writers. The error means another connection holds the
write lock when yours tried to upgrade. Mitigations:

- Open the database with @c PRAGMA @c journal_mode=WAL to allow readers
  while a writer is active.
- Use @c BEGIN @c IMMEDIATE instead of plain @c BEGIN to acquire the
  write lock at transaction start.
- Avoid sharing a single SQLite connection across threads — give each
  thread its own @c database_manager.

@section ts_orm ORM Entity Mapping Errors

@subsection ts_orm_metadata Symptom: empty fields() or missing columns in CREATE TABLE

The static @c metadata_ instance was never populated. Causes and fixes:

1. **Forgot to call @c add_field**. Every @c ENTITY_FIELD has a matching
   @c \<name\>_metadata_ member that must be added inside
   @c initialize_metadata().
2. **Two entities sharing the same static metadata**. Each subclass needs
   its own @c "static @c inline @c entity_metadata @c metadata_{...}"
   member with a unique table name.
3. **@c initialize_metadata() never ran**. Make sure you used the
   @c ENTITY_METADATA() macro inside the class — it wires up the static
   initializer that calls @c initialize_metadata().

@code{.cpp}
class user_entity : public entity_base
{
public:
    ENTITY_FIELD(int64_t,     id,
                 field_constraint::primary_key | field_constraint::auto_increment)
    ENTITY_FIELD(std::string, email, field_constraint::not_null | field_constraint::unique)
    ENTITY_METADATA()
private:
    static inline entity_metadata metadata_{"users"};
};

void user_entity::initialize_metadata()
{
    metadata_.add_field(id_metadata_);
    metadata_.add_field(email_metadata_);   // <-- easy to forget
}
@endcode

@subsection ts_orm_constraint Symptom: "NOT NULL constraint failed" on insert

A field marked @c not_null was left unset, or its value defaulted to
@c null because the entity was constructed without assignment. Always
assign every required field before calling save/insert, and consider
adding a static factory that takes mandatory fields as constructor
arguments.

@section ts_perf Query Performance Issues

@subsection ts_perf_slow Symptom: a SELECT that "should be fast" is slow

Run the backend's @c EXPLAIN tool to see the execution plan. Common
fixes:

- **Missing index.** Add @c field_constraint::index to the entity field
  used in the @c WHERE clause and re-run @c create_indexes_sql().
- **Implicit type conversion.** Comparing an indexed @c BIGINT column
  against a string literal forces a sequential scan. Use the typed
  query builder values (@c int64_t{...}) so the dialect formats them
  correctly.
- **Returning too many columns.** Replace @c SELECT @c * with an
  explicit projection. The query builder makes this trivial.
- **N+1 problem.** If you call @c select_query_result inside a loop,
  rewrite the work as a single JOIN or @c IN query.

@subsection ts_perf_pool Symptom: throughput drops as concurrency rises

You are likely contending on the driver-level pool. Increase
@c max_connections / @c maximumPoolSize on the driver, distribute the
load across more @c database_manager instances, or move the workload
behind the proxy mode (when available).

Use @c monitoring_system metrics from
@c database/monitoring/performance_monitor.h to confirm where time is
being spent.

@section ts_migrate Migration Conflicts

@subsection ts_migrate_drift Symptom: "column already exists" or "relation does not exist"

Your migration script and the live schema are out of sync. To recover:

1. Inspect the schema (@c "\\d @c users" in psql, @c DESCRIBE @c users
   in MySQL, @c db.users.findOne() in mongosh).
2. Compare against the migration that should have produced it.
3. Either re-run the missing migration or write a corrective migration
   that brings the live schema back in line.
4. If you regenerate DDL with @c entity::get_metadata().create_table_sql(),
   prefer @c CREATE @c TABLE @c IF @c NOT @c EXISTS so the script remains
   idempotent.

@subsection ts_migrate_dialect Symptom: works on PostgreSQL, fails on MySQL or SQLite

The DDL is using a dialect-specific feature. Examples:

- @c JSONB exists only in PostgreSQL.
- @c SERIAL is PostgreSQL-only; use @c BIGSERIAL or per-backend
  auto-increment.
- @c "CREATE @c INDEX @c IF @c NOT @c EXISTS" is supported by SQLite and
  PostgreSQL but not by older MySQL versions.

When you want a single script to run everywhere, drive the schema from
the ORM (@c create_table_sql) so the dialect translation happens
centrally, or keep separate per-backend migration files.

@section ts_more Still stuck?

- @ref faq — General-purpose answers
- @ref tutorial_quickstart — Verified end-to-end example
- File an issue at https://github.com/kcenon/database_system/issues with
  the failing call site, the @c result.error() output, and your CMake
  flags.

*/
